"""Original supporting fiction, commissioned by the approved AC04/AC5 plan.

The clinic, staff conversations, briefings and policy deliberations are authored
fiction, not quotations from either game. TNO/TNO:R were read as craft references.
Canon framing and transcript limitations are recorded separately in sources.json.
"""

NATIONAL = {
'ERU':[
"The officer who signs an occupation order will rarely see the kitchen in which it is read. On his map the district has a boundary and a reporting time. In the kitchen it has a mother who must cross that boundary, an injured neighbor, and a shopkeeper deciding whether tomorrow's bread is worth another argument at the checkpoint. Civil guarantees require the command to discipline its own men. Military discretion is easier to issue and harder to recall. Either decision leaves a record that survives the campaign. The preparation branch authorizes specific work; it cannot conjure the cooperation that the garrison has spent months consuming.",
"The maintenance chief knows precisely how many sorties the briefing requires. She also knows which aircraft has returned twice with the same fault. An argument about a spare part becomes an argument about loyalty as soon as a superior enters the hangar. Preserving the reserve means disappointing the operations room while there is still time to do so. Immediate readiness carries a different cost, which cannot be paid by the man who merely writes the number on a board. On the next shift, another mechanic will read the release entry and decide whether the signature beneath it can still be trusted.",
"A retreat can preserve an army, a reputation, or a population, and it seldom preserves all three. The roads on the staff map narrow when they reach the fuel returns. Local officials have their own lists of people who cannot leave. The command can authorize a withdrawal with its surviving cadre or demand another defense from the same tired formations. The choice is made while the front still exists. A later surrender cannot manufacture the arrangements that were refused here.",
"The first petition after the settlement bears an occupation stamp beside the new authority's seal. Nobody knows which of the two will trouble the receiving clerk more. Victory, defeat and an armistice leave different powers in different hands, but they all leave someone holding an order that once seemed sufficient. The account hears the cases produced by the actual campaign. It preserves the distinction between a negotiated access order and an unqualified promise of protection. Rebuilding begins when the administration can admit what it can presently do, and what it failed to do when the people asking were still inside the cordon."],
'FCU':[
"The allied table has more chairs than the room was designed to hold. Each chair represents a government that can say no, and a staff officer has already suggested removing the empty ones from the photograph. Shared authority makes command slower and the commitments more credible. Unified direction gives the operations staff clearer answers while concentrating responsibility for mistakes. The coalition needs a command it can use and an agreement its members will honor when the losses cease to be hypothetical. The first reconnaissance operation begins with those obligations rather than with a speech about unity.",
"The duty controller has learned to distinguish an aircraft that is late from a government that has stopped answering. Both appear as a blank space in the morning return. The rear bases can receive only so much, and the harbor offensive competes with the next replacement flight for crews and attention. A partner's acceptance is recorded separately from the offer. The command cannot assume that a refusal means disloyalty, or that an unanswered request secretly means yes. Its surviving air force must do the work that the timetable proposes.",
"The engineers arrive carrying bags that look too small for the importance attached to them. Their families do not appear on the technical diagram. Protecting both is part of the operation, and so is deciding how much to risk for information that the command has not yet confirmed. A continental foothold needs land actually controlled. The battery strike needs the preparations that make it possible. The focus branch authorizes these operations in sequence, leaving failure visible and the next commander obliged to deal with it.",
"There is no useful column for the silence after the victory announcement. The command has plans for demobilization and an estimate for the supervising mission, but the first delegation wants to know whether a particular road is open. Terms of victory determine who answers that question and who can appeal the answer. The standing account returns to the people encountered during the campaign. It leaves an occupation agreement, an evacuation record and a refused request recognizable as different things, even after the flags outside headquarters have changed."],
'OSE':[
"A verified channel is a modest thing to defend in a committee room. It has no launch ceremony and offers little for a photograph. Its value lies in knowing who received a message, who changed its wording, and whether the answer belonged to the government whose seal it carried. Public accountability exposes the channel to political scrutiny. A protected investigation shields its sources while increasing the burden on the people entrusted to judge them. Neither arrangement supplies the other government's consent. That answer must still arrive from the other side of the ocean.",
"At the receiving field, the fuel crew has been told to expect a quiet week. The operations officer cannot bring himself to repeat the instruction. A carrier and an island depend on different kinds of endurance: one can turn away, the other must find another runway and another place to put the wounded. Preparation authorizes real defensive and maritime work. Wardog's story follows the commitments that were made, the crews held in reserve, and the requests that could not be answered in time.",
"The disputed order is less dramatic than the investigator expected. It contains no confession, only a time that cannot be reconciled with the original message. To keep it means protecting the people who noticed. To disclose it means choosing an audience while the fighting continues. Rescue coverage, evidence custody and support for a real foothold compete for the same administrative attention. The branch makes those commitments available without pretending that a clean report repairs the damage already done to confidence inside the squadron.",
"The president's return is a beginning rather than an order that every unit will obey. The crews need an authenticated command, the investigators need to keep their evidence intact, and the other government needs a reason to believe that a reciprocal promise can survive the next broadcast. The final interception remains an operation with its own conditions. Peace requires both governments to accept current terms in a war that this campaign can actually settle. Its conclusion records the people and forces that survived to hear it."],
'YUK':[
"The officer asks for confirmation a second time. Across the desk, his superior closes the folder without answering. There are procedures for a damaged message, procedures for a delayed message, and considerably fewer procedures for the possibility that an intact message should not have been sent. Civilian scrutiny makes the dispute visible. Professional safeguards entrust a smaller circle with keeping an independent copy. Either course costs influence that might otherwise have purchased another convenient assurance from command.",
"A submerged carrier leaves little room for the language of public ceremonies. The crew knows the distance to its next receiving point and the work required to keep an aircraft ready inside a vessel that the sea is trying to close. Maritime denial can impose a terrible cost on an enemy while consuming the crews ordered to maintain it. A recall preserves a strategic asset and relinquishes its immediate threat. Neither outcome happens because the calendar has reached the date printed in a history book.",
"The receiving line begins with an officer trying to borrow a pencil. Beyond his table, families wait beside a vehicle that will not start. The invasion has made the distinction between front and rear depend on the hour, and the orderly cannot promise that the same road will be open tomorrow. Supporting the homeland means accepting a burden that does not vanish when the military situation improves. The review distinguishes an actual evacuation effort from a report prepared to reassure a distant ministry.",
"The political voice returning to public life must answer people who obeyed the army and people who suffered because it was obeyed. An authenticated request to stop fighting is necessary, but soldiers also need to know who stands behind the answer from Osea. The branch restores the capacity to make that decision and records the terms actually accepted. A refusal is a refusal, not a concealed route to reconciliation. A peace that survives begins with both governments accepting responsibility for an agreement they can presently enforce."],
'BLK':[
"The settlement files contain the country that actually emerged from the earlier war. A conventional defeat, a narrow peace and an alternate victory cannot all produce the same veterans' meeting. The clerk lays out the surviving orders and waits while the visitors decide which parts they would prefer not to remember. Opening the archive commits the government to hearing a record that can contradict its most useful story. Closing it leaves that story available to people whose authority depends on nobody asking what the documents say.",
"The government possesses buildings, ranks and a legal chain of responsibility. A network possesses favors that can outlive the office in which they were granted. They sometimes employ the same people. Protecting an independent investigation weakens the claim that national loyalty requires silence. Supporting the channel spends public capacity on an authority that will not be accountable through ordinary institutions. The player chooses which work receives protection. The resulting operations preserve the difference between the state, its population, and the people acting in secret.",
"A procurement file can look innocent because every page has the correct signature. Its danger lies in the relationships between the pages. Influence can move a request through several offices without any one clerk possessing the whole plan. Extending that chain creates access and leaves evidence. Protecting a witness can give foreign investigators an independent point of comparison, at the cost of exposing what the government preferred to keep domestic. Both operations have consequences outside Belka without declaring a conventional national war.",
"When the crisis reaches its final days, access is less useful than control and control is less durable than its operators believe. A network may seize a command channel while an investigation is still piecing together its existence. It may also find that a witness survived, that a second copy was preserved, or that another government learned to verify a message before acting on it. The reckoning records what the network achieved and what the state tolerated. Its alternate success is explicit authored gameplay; it is not an inevitable verdict on Belka or its people."],
'SNS':["The clinic keeps its old opening hours because changing them would make the war feel permanent. That is what the nurse says, and the porter lets her say it. They both know that most mornings begin with a different list of things they no longer have. Continuity means finding a practical arrangement with powers that can close the road. Protected local administration means deciding which compromises can be recorded without endangering the people who made them. The branch turns that choice into a receiving operation whose outcome returns in the occupation story and, if the city is liberated, in the work of rebuilding."],
'NPT':["The arriving aircraft are only the visible part of the commitment. Someone has to receive their crews, clear a hangar, keep the communications staff awake, and tell the neighborhood that another building has been requisitioned. A dispersed receiving plan protects capacity by making the headquarters less convenient. A fortified headquarters offers command a firmer center while concentrating its demands. The branch asks for an actual allied acceptance before it records a hosting commitment. The island's government retains its own responsibility for the people already living beneath the flight path."],
'AMB':["The reserve ledger has names in its margins. A driver whose replacement has not arrived, a depot supervisor whose daughter is due home, and a district that has already lent everything it can spare. Publishing an allocation makes its limits visible to everyone who will be disappointed by them. A protected schedule gives command greater discretion while placing more responsibility on the people receiving the report. The transport offer is bounded, funded and subject to a current answer from the coalition. Its costs remain real after a refusal, because preparing a credible commitment was itself work."],
'UGE':["The second receiving site is a collection of roofs, a water connection and a promise that the district engineer considers optimistic. Dispersing capacity offers room to survive the loss of one site. Concentrating it makes the current service easier to operate and the next disruption harder to absorb. There is no arrangement that creates staff from the lines on a map. The branch funds a specific receiving commitment and records whether the coalition actually accepts it. Its domestic opportunity cost remains attached to the government that authorized the work."],
'DEL':["The inspector recognizes the signature before she recognizes the error. It belongs to an officer who was helpful during the last crisis, and that makes asking for the original file more difficult. Independent inspection preserves a route around the command that issued the order. Command inspection can obtain faster cooperation while narrowing the people allowed to dispute its conclusions. The operational docket records a defined service to the coalition. It cannot erase the unresolved cases that made the inspection necessary, or treat an unanswered foreign request as an acceptance."],
'SBK':["The residents have learned that a familiar surname can turn a routine question into an interview. A civil file records conduct, dates and witnesses. A rumor can dispense with all three. Protecting civil procedures takes authority away from people who promise quick certainty. A security review keeps more discretion inside the administration while making the integrity of the individual investigator harder to replace. The campaign preserves specific evidence and commitments without making the surrounding population responsible for the existence of a covert network."],
'SAP':["The minister's map shows an access corridor. The local authority's copy shows schools, a hospital and the bridge on which the morning buses queue. A parliamentary mandate makes those competing obligations public before the offer is sent. A limited executive mandate offers a faster answer with a narrower circle responsible for its enforcement. The commitment requires a receiving government's present acceptance and can be refused. Its conclusion records the mandate that was used rather than granting an alliance, a guarantee or an automatic entry into war."],
'UST':["The veterans listening to the briefing have heard the word temporary before. Some remember who used it and some remember the road they were standing on when they heard it. Strictly limited support offers a smaller commitment whose boundaries are easier to defend. A conditional mission permits more useful work while requiring the government to maintain a credible power to withdraw. The receiving country must answer. Ustio's earlier settlement, sovereignty and original cast are retained; the new branch does not rewrite them to make a convenient mission available."],
'GEB':["A transit agreement is tested by the first vehicle whose papers do not match its cargo. Published rules give the border staff an answer they can show to a foreign officer. Escorted transit places more responsibility on the personnel accompanying the movement. Both arrangements occupy people who might otherwise be maintaining the domestic network. The offer goes to a present receiving authority, and the recorded outcome distinguishes preparation, consent and completion. A road does not become an alliance merely because an official has drawn an arrow along it."],
'REC':["The finance office has prepared two estimates. One assumes that the next request will be the last. The other has been circulated less widely. Domestic recovery first preserves a clearer limit on what can be promised abroad. A bounded contribution accepts a smaller reserve at home in exchange for a defined role in the regional effort. Neither policy entitles the government to resources it has not funded. The branch sends an offer whose receiver can refuse, preserves its political cost, and leaves the domestic consequences visible after the diplomatic photograph has been filed."],
'FAT':["The council secretary has placed the disputed clause on a separate sheet. It is easier to postpone a sentence when nobody has to look at the rest of the agreement around it. Unanimous consultation preserves every member's opportunity to object. A delegated mandate can deliver a faster answer while narrowing the work the council has expressly authorized. The branch receives the current offers and keeps their permissions separate. It does not create guarantees, foreign bases, faction membership or a shared willingness to fight out of the convenience of one meeting."],
}

SCENES=[]


def scene(ident,tag,title,body,gate,options=None,glyph='document'):
    SCENES.append(dict(id=ident,tag=tag,title=title,body=body.strip(),gate=gate,
        options=options or [dict(label='Keep this account.')],glyph=glyph))


def done(tag,ident):
    return f'{tag} = {{ has_country_flag = ac_camp_scene_{ident}_resolved }}'


OCCUPIED='has_global_flag = ac_ucw_war_started 310 = { is_controlled_by = ERU }'
scene(1,'SNS','The hours on the door',"""
The porter had taken the opening hours down twice. Both times the nurse put them back before the first patient arrived. The paper was yellowing at the corners. Beneath it, someone had scratched the old municipal crest with a key and then, apparently ashamed of the effort, tried to rub the mark away.

There were six people waiting when the truck failed to appear. By noon there were twenty-three. The man from the bakery brought his own stool. A woman stood with a sleeping child and refused the chair offered to her, as though sitting down would acknowledge that nobody knew how long this would take.

The nurse laid the stockbook before the local administrator. She had crossed out the names of medicines already exhausted. What remained looked reassuring until he understood that she had begun listing the substitutes beneath them. The porter listened from the doorway. Outside, an occupation patrol was stopping a cyclist. The soldier held the bicycle while his partner read the man's papers with the unhurried concentration of someone whose own appointment could not be missed.

"I can ask for a vehicle," the administrator said.

"Then ask."

He looked at the municipal stamp beside the stockbook. Requests bearing that stamp went into an office run by the people who had closed the road. Going there might bring the medicines. It would certainly create a file.

The nurse turned the book around so he could read the last column. She had titled it Patients expected tomorrow. There were no numbers in it yet.
""",OCCUPIED,[dict(label='Put the request in writing.',effect='set_global_flag = ac_camp_clinic_civil_permission'),dict(label='Keep the patient list outside their files.',effect='set_global_flag = ac_camp_clinic_civil_permission\nset_global_flag = ac_camp_clinic_private_list')],'medical')

scene(2,'ERU','An exception in triplicate',"""
The request arrived with the wrong kind of stamp. The duty officer noticed that first because it was the one problem he could solve without telephoning anyone. He sent an orderly for the proper form. The municipal administrator remained standing, holding his hat low enough to hide the dust on it.

There was a clinic on the officer's district map. A small cross, carefully placed so that it did not touch the line marking a restricted road. He had assumed the cross represented a working institution. Most things on the map represented working institutions until somebody came through the door to explain otherwise.

"What is in the vehicle?"

"Medicines. Dressings. Whatever the depot can release."

"And who will accompany it?"

The administrator named a nurse and then stopped. The officer waited. A second name would make the request more complete. It might also make it impossible for the person named to walk home without explaining why his papers had suddenly become interesting.

The telephone rang on the next desk. Somebody was demanding another transport return for headquarters. The officer could authorize a limited passage, subject to inspection, and spend the afternoon explaining the exception to people who would never see the clinic. Or he could retain the existing restrictions and tell the administrator to submit a correct request through the proper office.

The orderly returned with three blank copies. He placed them between the men and asked which name should go on the receipt.
""",OCCUPIED+' '+done('SNS',1),[dict(label='Authorize inspected medical passage.',effect='set_global_flag = ac_camp_clinic_military_permission'),dict(label='Maintain the existing restriction.',effect='set_global_flag = ac_camp_clinic_restricted')],'security')

scene(3,'FCU','A message without a target',"""
The intelligence officer had almost put the message in the wrong tray. It named a road, an expected movement, and an occupation checkpoint. Three useful things, if one ignored what the sender was actually asking.

The liaison read it a second time. A municipal clinic needed a transport arrangement. There was no request for weapons and no offer of a raid. Someone inside the occupied district wanted the allied command to know that a particular journey was supposed to remain a medical journey.

Around the table, the morning briefing continued. A reconnaissance flight had returned with photographs of a depot. Another had not returned at all. The duty controller was arguing for a replacement aircraft that the operations staff had already promised elsewhere. The liaison waited until the argument paused long enough to become a question.

"If we acknowledge this, who hears the acknowledgment?"

Nobody could answer completely. The local contact had protected the names he could protect. The enemy controlled the road. An allied assurance could help the driver at one checkpoint and make him a suspect at the next.

The officer moved the message away from the targeting folder. That was a decision of sorts, although nobody would write it up as one. A corridor existed first as a series of instructions that the people carrying weapons had agreed to remember. The command could protect the separation between relief and intelligence work. It could also keep the information available for military use and tell itself that no formal promise had been made.

Outside the room, a clerk waited to collect the completed tasking sheets.
""",OCCUPIED+' '+done('ERU',2),[dict(label='Protect and sponsor the receiving route.',effect='custom_effect_tooltip = ac_camp_clinic_sponsor_cost\nset_global_flag = ac_camp_clinic_protected_contact\nset_global_flag = ac_camp_clinic_sponsorship'),dict(label='Retain the message for operational use.',effect='set_global_flag = ac_camp_clinic_exposed_contact\nif = { limit = { NOT = { SNS = { has_country_flag = ac_camp_relief_active } } } set_global_flag = ac_camp_relief_cancelled }')],'radar')

scene(4,'SNS','The name in the margin',"""
The pharmacist recognized the handwriting before she read the name. It belonged to a woman who had once corrected every spelling error in the municipal circular, including the mayor's. Beside her request for information was the careful addition: My brother has not returned.

The administrator could remember the brother's face more readily than his address. A delivery driver, usually cheerful, with a habit of leaving his cap on the counter while he searched for a receipt. He might have been delayed, detained, injured, or persuaded that a different road offered a better chance of getting home. None of those possibilities could be entered in the column marked Current location.

The new medical passage had acquired its own list of questions. Who would drive? Who would inspect? Which names could be shown? The pharmacist had already been asked whether the vehicle might carry a message. She had answered that a message weighed very little, and had regretted the answer as soon as she saw the relief in the woman's face.

Now the paper lay between them. Adding a missing person's inquiry would turn the route into something more complicated than the garrison had been asked to authorize. Refusing it would mean explaining, in the language of a functioning administration, why a family should wait without asking.

The pharmacist folded the letter along its existing crease. The name disappeared inside it. She could keep a private copy and pursue the inquiry separately, or add the request to the official medical file, where somebody with access to the occupation records might finally read it.
""",OCCUPIED+' '+done('FCU',3),[dict(label='Keep a private inquiry beside the medical file.',effect='set_global_flag = ac_camp_clinic_private_inquiry'),dict(label='Submit the name through the official channel.',effect='set_global_flag = ac_camp_clinic_official_inquiry')],'document')

scene(5,'ERU','The purpose of the search',"""
The sergeant had arranged the contents of the stopped vehicle on a tarpaulin. He was proud of the orderliness. Boxes of dressings stood in one row, bottles in another, and a small bag of personal belongings occupied a space of its own. The driver watched the bag rather than the medicines.

The duty officer read the search return. There was no contraband. There was a letter, which had therefore become the most interesting object present. The sergeant had placed it beneath a clean stone to keep the wind from turning it over.

"It could be a code."

"Anything could be a code."

The answer sounded sharper than the officer intended. Behind him, a corporal was repeating the vehicle's permit number into a radio that would not give a clear reply. The delay had become a reason for further delay. Every additional question demonstrated the diligence of the men asking it while making the original permission a little less useful.

The officer could limit the search to the authority stated on the permit. That would mean returning the letter unread and taking responsibility for what he had chosen not to know. He could also retain it for examination, release the medicines, and report that the route remained open. Both accounts would be technically defensible in the office receiving them.

The driver asked whether he could put the bag back in the cab. He addressed the question to the sergeant, because that was the man holding it. The officer realized that neither of them was waiting for an explanation of policy.
""",OCCUPIED+' '+done('SNS',4),[dict(label='Search within the written authority.',effect='set_global_flag = ac_camp_clinic_search_limited\nadd_timed_idea = { idea = ac_camp_garrison_limits days = 90 }'),dict(label='Retain the correspondence for examination.',effect='set_global_flag = ac_camp_clinic_letter_retained\nadd_timed_idea = { idea = ac_camp_retained_correspondence days = 90 }')],'security')

scene(6,'FCU','The aircraft already promised',"""
The liaison found the controller eating from a tin balanced on the corner of his desk. The food had gone cold before he opened it. On the wall, the serviceability board had acquired a second column headed Possible, which made the first column look less discouraging until one read what was in it.

The request was modest on paper. A receiving contact needed confirmation that the medical movement had not been folded into an offensive tasking. Someone also wanted a report on the road beyond the district. Neither request involved a strike, but both involved attention that had already been assigned elsewhere.

"You want an aircraft?"

"I want a reliable answer."

The controller looked at the board. Those were often the same thing, and the difference was becoming a luxury. An additional reconnaissance commitment could help establish what was happening around the route. It would also expose the contact to the suspicion that the convoy was serving a military purpose. Keeping the arrangements separate offered less information and a clearer obligation.

He put down the tin and drew a line through one penciled suggestion. There was no shortage of ideas that used an aircraft which had not yet been repaired. What remained were choices between people who had already been told that their work mattered.

The liaison waited while he wrote the reply. It would travel back through several hands. By the time it reached the clinic, a short sentence about the limits of allied assistance would have become the only part of this argument anybody there could use.
""",OCCUPIED+' '+done('ERU',5),[dict(label='Send a precise statement of the limits.',effect='set_global_flag = ac_camp_clinic_honest_limits'),dict(label='Reserve additional observation.',gate='check_variable = { deployed_airforce_manpower_k > 0 } check_variable = { num_deployed_planes > 0 }',effect='set_global_flag = ac_camp_clinic_conditional_watch\nadd_timed_idea = { idea = ac_camp_observation_assignment days = 90 }')],'communications')

scene(7,'SNS','A chair by the window',"""
The baker left his stool at the clinic. He said carrying it home and back again was becoming an inconvenience. The nurse thanked him without pointing out that he had begun bringing it only because the waiting room had lost half its chairs to a requisition.

By the window, the woman who had asked about her brother was repairing a tear in the child's sleeve. She had returned with a different question. Did the clinic know where a person should go if a letter had been taken at a checkpoint? The pharmacist said she would find out. She wrote the question in the private notebook, below the earlier question about a man who had not come home.

The stockbook still occupied the administrator's desk. Its empty columns had acquired dates. A promise was useful only until the people waiting began to organize their lives around it, and then it became a responsibility that someone had to carry even if the vehicle never arrived.

The porter checked the opening hours before he unlocked the door. Outside, a child was tracing a line through the dust with a broken piece of tile. The line crossed the clinic steps and continued toward the checkpoint. When the patrol appeared, he rubbed it away with his shoe.

"You can leave that," the porter said, too late for the child to believe him.

He went back inside and moved the baker's stool to the window. There would be someone waiting there tomorrow, whether the receiving route worked or not.
""",OCCUPIED+' '+done('FCU',6),glyph='medical')

scene(8,'ERU','The report that fits',"""
Headquarters had asked for a shorter report. The officer removed a paragraph about the search delay and discovered that the remaining account described a much more successful arrangement. The permit had been issued. A vehicle had passed. No prohibited goods had been found. Three facts, none of them false, and none of them sufficient to explain why the municipal administrator was waiting outside again.

The sergeant brought the correspondence file. It had become thicker because several offices had made their own copies of the same letter. A clerk had underlined the word brother and added a question mark. Another had written No identified military significance in the margin. The officer wondered whether either man had understood that the writer might have considered that the point.

He returned to the transport account. A limited exception could survive only if the next shift knew its limits. The duty roster changed more often than the instructions. Someone would eventually stop the driver because he had never seen this particular form, and everyone concerned would have a defensible explanation afterward.

The administrator came in when called. He had brought no new request. Instead he laid a copy of the original authorization on the desk, worn along the fold and discolored where it had been handled in the rain.

"Could the next patrol be told?"

The officer looked at the shorter report and then at the paper on his desk. The exception needed work precisely because the report said that it had already worked.
""",OCCUPIED+' '+done('SNS',7),glyph='document')

scene(9,'FCU','The space outside the map',"""
The intelligence section had annotated the district map so thoroughly that the liaison borrowed a clean copy. On it, the clinic was merely a building. The road had no colored arrows. For a moment the absence of military significance looked like a form of peace.

The latest message contained a stock return and a request that nobody include the driver's name in the next circulation. The liaison could not tell which part of the arrangement had frightened him. A checkpoint, a letter, a conversation with a neighbor: the command received effects more readily than causes.

A staff officer asked whether the route could support anything larger. The question was reasonable. That made refusing to answer it require a better reason than discomfort. The liaison explained what had been offered and what had not. He heard himself becoming precise in the way people become precise when they are trying to preserve a promise that others consider inconvenient.

The officer nodded and asked for the information to be marked accordingly. A note would not protect a convoy by itself. It would, however, oblige the next person to know that a boundary existed before deciding to cross it.

The liaison placed the clean map inside the medical file. There was no new military target to report. There were people expecting a particular vehicle to remain a particular kind of vehicle. Keeping that expectation intact had become work for a command fighting a war it could not afford to lose.
""",OCCUPIED+' '+done('ERU',8),glyph='radar')

scene(10,'SNS','What the children heard',"""
The school reopened in two rooms because the other rooms had broken windows. The teacher divided the children by the number of chairs rather than by age. On the first morning, she asked them to write down what they had done the previous day. Half of them wrote about standing in a queue. One described a truck with a cracked mirror. Another left the page empty and drew a careful border around it.

At midday the nurse came to collect a child whose mother was waiting at the clinic. The teacher followed her into the corridor and asked whether there had been an accident. The nurse said no. Then she explained that no was sometimes the only answer she could give without making the waiting harder.

They stood beside the door that had once led to the music room. Someone had moved the piano against the wall to protect it from the weather. A patrol went past outside. The children fell quiet without being told to do so, and the teacher waited until the footsteps had faded before opening the classroom again.

The nurse had brought a small box of dressings from the clinic, or what remained of one. She left it on the teacher's desk with a list of instructions for conserving them. The list was written on the back of an outdated municipal notice.

After she left, the teacher turned the notice over. The old side announced an ordinary repair to the water supply. She read it twice before putting the instructions face up again.
""",OCCUPIED+' '+done('FCU',9),glyph='shelter')

scene(11,'ERU','The returning patrol',"""
The patrol returned with one fewer man than it had taken out. The missing soldier was at a medical post, and the officer wrote that down before any rumor could improve on it. Around the desk, the others described the incident in the language of people who had each seen a different part of the same street.

The sergeant wanted to close the road until the report was complete. The officer asked whether closing it would make the report more accurate. Nobody answered immediately. A restriction could be justified by uncertainty and could also ensure that uncertainty lasted until the next shift found another reason to retain it.

On the edge of the desk lay the clinic authorization. It had begun as an exception. The officer had since discovered that exceptions generated more correspondence than ordinary rules because somebody had to keep choosing to honor them. The administrator's latest note contained no appeal to compassion. It gave the time of the next receiving movement and asked which instructions the checkpoint should expect.

The officer telephoned the medical post. The injured soldier was awake and complaining about the food. He asked whether his unit had been told. The officer said it had, although technically he was still doing so.

When he hung up, the sergeant was waiting beside the district map. Beyond the window, the evening patrol was forming in the yard. The same street would receive them. The people living on it would have to discover whether the previous day's permission still meant anything.
""",OCCUPIED+' '+done('SNS',10),glyph='security')

scene(12,'FCU','The promise that remained',"""
The liaison found the first message while clearing the file for review. Its wording had acquired an innocence through repetition. A clinic, a road, an expected vehicle. Someone had underlined the receiving time and penciled a question beside the proposed contact. Later messages answered some questions and made others impossible to ask in the same way.

He laid them out in order. There were permissions, restrictions, a stock return, an account of a delay, and a carefully phrased request about a person who had not come home. The military summaries had described the route as a secondary matter. Its papers now covered most of his desk.

Outside, a replacement crew was receiving a briefing. Their controller spoke with the calm insistence of a man who had explained the same dangers to too many new faces. The liaison listened until the door closed. Then he began writing the account for the people who would inherit this file.

He recorded the offers as offers and the answers as answers. An unanswered question remained unanswered. The clinic had not become an allied installation because its staff had sought help. A garrison permission had not established that every search honored it. A vehicle's arrival could be recorded without claiming that the people waiting had ceased to be afraid.

At the bottom he left space for the operation's result. He would not fill it from the intended schedule.

The next page was reserved for the city after the front. It was still blank. For once, a blank page seemed an honest promise.
""",OCCUPIED+' '+done('ERU',11),glyph='document')

scene(13,'SNS','The first delivery',"""
The truck arrived after the porter had stopped looking for it. He was carrying the baker's stool back into the waiting room when the engine sounded beyond the corner. For an instant nobody moved. Then the nurse was outside, reading the labels before the driver had finished telling her which boxes had been opened.

There was less than the stockbook had requested. There was also a carton the depot had added because someone there had recognized the clinic's name. The pharmacist put that carton aside until she could discover whether it contained what the label promised. Experience had made gratitude a careful procedure.

The driver wanted a receipt. The administrator wrote one, made a copy and asked whether he had been delayed. The driver looked toward the street before answering. He described the road, the search and the people he had spoken to. The administrator recorded the delays separately from the permission. A successful arrival did not make either of them disappear.

By the window, the woman waiting for news of her brother asked whether there had been a letter. The driver said there had been several papers. He did not know whether any of them was the paper she meant. She thanked him for looking, although he had not said that he had looked.

Inside, the nurse crossed two items off the list of substitutes. She left the opening hours where they were. The clinic would open tomorrow with a little more to offer, and with the same people bringing the questions the medicines could not answer.
""",'has_global_flag = ac_camp_relief_success '+done('FCU',12),glyph='medical')

scene(14,'SNS','An arrangement with a locked door',"""
The notice was written in the familiar municipal typeface. That made the additional restriction look like something the town had decided for itself. The administrator read it aloud to the pharmacist and stopped before the final line. It required a receiving signature from an office whose staff had been sent home.

The route had not become wholly impossible. It had become possible on terms that prevented the clinic from using it when it was most needed. A vehicle could leave on the approved day if the driver was available, the papers were renewed, and the people authorized to confirm them had not been assigned elsewhere. Each condition belonged to a different desk. No single person had refused the whole request.

The pharmacist folded the notice into the stockbook. She had begun writing smaller to make the remaining space last. Outside, the baker was arguing with a supplier about the meaning of next week. His voice rose, then fell when he remembered how far it could carry along the street.

The nurse asked whether the arrangement should still be called open. The administrator looked at the signature line. There was an answer that would satisfy the next report and another answer that would help the patients decide what to bring with them.

"Tell them to bring what they have," he said.

The porter checked the door before leaving. He did not take the opening hours down. They had become a statement about the people inside rather than about the reliability of the road.
""",'OR = { has_global_flag = ac_camp_relief_limited has_global_flag = ac_camp_relief_cancelled has_global_flag = ac_camp_relief_interrupted } '+done('FCU',12),glyph='gate')

scene(15,'SNS','The column left empty',"""
The deadline passed during an ordinary afternoon. There was no announcement. The porter heard the nurse turn a page in the stockbook and knew, from the care with which she did it, that she had stopped expecting the truck.

The administrator went to the depot office again. The clerk found his reference number in a folder whose label had changed since the previous visit. The request was complete. The permissions, where they existed, had been copied. What nobody could presently supply was a journey that satisfied all the conditions at once.

He came back with the original papers because leaving them there had begun to feel like abandoning the people whose names were on them. The pharmacist asked whether she should keep a copy. He said yes. They both understood that keeping a copy could not fill a prescription. They did it anyway, because one day somebody might ask why the receiving column had remained empty.

By the window, the woman put her sewing away. She asked whether the clinic would open the following morning. The nurse answered before the administrator could begin explaining the arrangement.

"At the usual time."

After the patients left, the porter moved the stool back beneath the window. He had started doing it every evening. The room would look prepared when the first person arrived, and preparedness was one small thing the people working there still had the power to provide.

The stockbook remained on the desk. Its blank column would survive the afternoon in which the promise had expired.
""",'has_global_flag = ac_camp_relief_failed '+done('FCU',12),glyph='document')

scene(16,'ERU','The account of an exception',"""
The officer's replacement arrived with a cleaner map and a complete set of district instructions. They spent the morning comparing the two. Near midday, the departing officer took the clinic file out of the transfer box and placed it separately on the desk.

"That one needs explaining."

His replacement opened it. The papers contained the route as it had been requested, the permissions that had been issued, the questions that had been retained, and the outcome entered when the operation closed. It was a less convenient record than the summary headquarters had asked for.

The younger man read the search reports twice. He wanted to know which instructions had been effective. The officer found himself answering with the names of people rather than the numbers of orders. A sergeant who had telephoned before holding a vehicle. An administrator who had returned with the same worn paper. A nurse who had refused to describe an empty shelf as a temporary inconvenience.

"And now?"

The officer looked at the cleaned-up map. A change of staff could make a functioning exception disappear more efficiently than a deliberate refusal. It could also reopen a restriction that everyone had begun to treat as unavoidable.

He wrote the receiving contact on the cover and left the original authorization inside. His replacement would have his own choices to make. At least those choices would not begin with the comforting assumption that the cross on the map represented a clinic whose doors opened by themselves.
""",'OR = { has_global_flag = ac_camp_relief_success has_global_flag = ac_camp_relief_limited has_global_flag = ac_camp_relief_failed has_global_flag = ac_camp_relief_cancelled has_global_flag = ac_camp_relief_interrupted } '+done('FCU',12),glyph='security')

scene(17,'FCU','A useful failure to simplify',"""
The review officer asked for a single conclusion. The liaison offered the operation result. That was not quite what had been requested. A conclusion was supposed to explain whether the arrangement should be repeated elsewhere, preferably in language that could be circulated without the accompanying file.

The liaison read the summary again. The route had required people in occupied territory to disclose information to one authority and ask another authority to respect the limits of its use. The result recorded what had actually happened to that attempt. It could not show that the next driver would receive the same answer at the same checkpoint.

He added a paragraph about the private inquiry. The review officer wondered whether it was relevant. The liaison said that the woman who had written it would probably consider it relevant. The conversation stopped for a moment, then continued more carefully.

Outside the office, a courier was waiting with dispatches for the next shift. The war had not paused for a medical arrangement, and the command had other duties it could not decline. That was a fact worth preserving beside the reasons the arrangement had needed protection in the first place.

In the end, the review circulated with an annex. The annex contained the limits of the commitment, the actual answers received, and the questions still awaiting an answer. Someone would complain about its length.

The liaison sent a copy to the receiving desk. The people inheriting the next request would at least know that a small line on their map might contain a whole afternoon of somebody else's life.
""",'OR = { has_global_flag = ac_camp_relief_success has_global_flag = ac_camp_relief_limited has_global_flag = ac_camp_relief_failed has_global_flag = ac_camp_relief_cancelled has_global_flag = ac_camp_relief_interrupted } '+done('FCU',12),glyph='document')

scene(30,'FCU','The harbor in the photograph',"""
The photograph had been enlarged until the paper showed a grain of its own. The targeting officer held it against the lamp while the naval liaison pointed out the shapes that mattered to his service. A ship was a hull in one report, a threat in another, and a crew in the letters that would arrive much later.

The operation had given the command a completed maritime targeting return. It had not supplied a license to describe every enemy vessel in the harbor as sunk. The naval war would provide that accounting through losses that could actually be observed. For now the staff could say which preparations had been made and what support was available for the next engagement.

One of the younger officers asked whether the photograph would be released. The liaison looked at the edges. There were streets outside the naval facilities, buildings whose roofs had no significance to the briefing, and a road that continued beyond the frame. He asked for a second copy with the targeting marks removed.

At the evening conference, the completed operation was entered in the campaign return. Its temporary advantage would have to be used while the crews and information remained current. Across the table, the representative from a partner government asked what had been spent from the receiving reserve to support it.

The answer took longer than the announcement. It included shifts, aircraft and commitments made by offices that would receive little mention in the history of the harbor. The liaison left the clean photograph on the table until the meeting ended.
""",'has_global_flag = ac_camp_fleet_success',glyph='navy')

scene(31,'FCU','The families on the technical brief',"""
The engineer asked for another chair before he asked to see the diagram. The intelligence officer had prepared for questions about the battery's targeting system. He had not prepared for a child to fall asleep against the chair's back while the adults worked.

The family had brought very little. Everything that could identify a route or a person had become a decision on the way out. Now a receiving clerk was asking them to supply the information again, in a different order, because the allied forms had different boxes.

The liaison stopped the interview long enough to obtain food. The engineer kept explaining through the interruption. He pointed to a part of the diagram and described what the people maintaining it had actually done. For the first time, the targeting officer could attach an ordinary working routine to a system that had dominated the air campaign.

Outside, an aircraft was being prepared for another duty. Its crew had been told that the operation would matter. The engineer heard the engine and lost his place. His wife put a hand beside the paper, waited, and moved the child's chair away from the open door.

The technical return would help make a strike possible. The families remained in the receiving account, where their protection could not be reduced to a footnote beneath the intelligence they had brought.

When the interview ended, the engineer asked whether the diagram could stay behind. The officer said that it could. The man stood up and, for the first time since arriving, took his hands away from it.
""",'has_global_flag = ac_camp_engineers_success',glyph='refugees')

scene(32,'FCU','A sky that must be used',"""
The controller repeated the completion message because he did not trust himself to have heard it correctly. Around him, the operations room continued its work for several seconds before the silence began to spread from desk to desk.

The battery strike had returned a verified result. The crews had carried the engineers' information into the sky and brought back an answer the operations room could use. That did not bring back the aircraft already lost beneath Stonehenge's reach, and it did not arrange the next offensive. It changed what the next crews could be asked to attempt.

The targeting officer took the engineering diagram down from the briefing board. Beneath it was an older chart, still marked with the limits that had governed so many plans. He left that one where it was until the next shift arrived. They would need to understand why the new orders were different.

An allied representative telephoned to ask about receiving arrangements for the counteroffensive. A maintenance officer asked whether the temporary dispersal sites were still required. A local authority wanted to know whether the air warning schedule could be relaxed. The victory entered ordinary life as a collection of questions, each requiring an answer from someone who could be held to it.

At the far end of the room, a clerk began copying the names of the crews committed to the strike. The controller watched him and thought of all the times the room had celebrated a plan before the aircraft returned.

This time there was a result to record. The work waiting beyond it had already begun to ring on the telephone.
""",'has_global_flag = ac_camp_battery_success',glyph='radar')

scene(40,'ERU','The aircraft left in the hangar',"""
The maintenance chief had expected another argument. She had kept the original fault return on the bench so that nobody could pretend the decision had been made from a single complaint. When the order finally arrived, it contained three plain words: Retain for repair.

Yellow Four had been furious about the decision before she knew what it would spare her. The paperwork described an aircraft withheld from a sortie, the maintenance hours required to make that decision useful, and an operational demand accepted elsewhere because somebody had agreed to disappoint the briefing room. The chief had signed the return while the argument was still about an aircraft, before it became an argument anybody wanted to have about a person.

The pilot read the return without thanking anyone immediately. Gratitude would have made the argument sound settled. There were still friends flying the missions she had not flown, and a squadron whose losses could not be moved into somebody else's column.

Outside the hangar, an officer was asking which reserve aircraft could be released next. The chief pointed him toward the serviceability board. Its figures were lower than the operations staff wanted. They were also figures she could sign.

The war would continue to ask what a pilot owed the people beside her. This branch had preserved a person at the cost of a capability the command had wanted to use. It left her alive to discover what that decision would mean after the reputation of the squadron and the fate of the country ceased to move together.
""",'has_global_flag = ac_camp_yellow_four_preserved has_global_flag = ac_camp_battery_success',glyph='tools')

scene(41,'ERU','The space beside the name',"""
The clerk drew a line through the next duty assignment before anyone told him which form to use. He had been keeping the squadron board long enough to recognize the kind of silence that entered the room ahead of an official return.

Yellow Four's name remained on the maintenance return. The aircraft had been required, the fault had been recorded, and the people who had argued about the difference would now remember the argument in different ways. The clerk could correct a number on the board. He could not think of a correction that would make the empty space beneath it bearable.

The maintenance chief came in to collect the original fault returns. A staff officer asked whether the review could wait until the current emergency had passed. She looked at him for a moment and asked which emergency he meant. There had been a current emergency every day the aircraft was required.

Yellow Thirteen stood beside the door while the clerk finished the entry. Nobody in the room could offer a phrase that would make it adequate. The squadron still had orders, and the command would require a serviceable force for the next operation. Those facts continued to exist with an offensive indifference to the empty place among them.

The chief took the papers back to the hangar. They would be needed by whoever inherited the work and by whoever was eventually asked to explain it. A record could preserve the sequence of decisions without pretending that the people making them had understood the sequence in advance.

The clerk left room beneath the name for the final return. He had become very careful about leaving room.
""",'has_global_flag = ac_camp_yellow_four_lost',glyph='document')

scene(42,'ERU','An order to come back',"""
The withdrawal order reached the unit while its officers were discussing how long the remaining fuel would last. Nobody believed the first reading. A second operator checked the authentication, then handed it back without looking at the others.

The withdrawal was authorized, supplied and authenticated. Yellow Thirteen would face the country's settlement alive. The order offered no answer to what had already happened to the squadron, and it did not restore the authority the unit had once enjoyed. It provided a route out of the last demand being made on it, a route somebody had spent time arranging while the command could still insist there was no reason to prepare one.

The officer preparing the return asked which equipment should receive priority. Someone answered too quickly. Someone else mentioned people who were still waiting outside the military receiving point. A withdrawal could be described as an orderly movement on paper while its practical limits were being argued over one vehicle at a time.

The pilot listened and then asked for the civilian receiving instructions. His presence had become another obligation for the people arranging the movement. Surviving a last stand was not the same as being excused from explaining why there had nearly been one.

At headquarters, the completed withdrawal was entered beside reports of formations that had not received the same opportunity. It would follow the command into victory, armistice or defeat.

The next chapter would have fewer opportunities for the language of legend. It would require an ordinary answer from a person who had been allowed to return.
""",'has_global_flag = ac_camp_yellow_thirteen_preserved ac_ucw_war_over = yes',glyph='gate')

scene(43,'SNS','The same window',"""
The first thing the porter noticed was that people had begun speaking at their ordinary volume. He stood on the clinic steps and listened to two neighbors disagree about where a vehicle should be parked. Neither lowered his voice when armed men passed at the end of the street.

The city was under San Salvacion's control again. The new patrol had a local authority to answer to, and the administrator could ask for its instructions without first explaining his own right to remain in the building. He brought the occupation file out of its box and placed it beside the new receiving instructions. There were permissions to compare, questions to pursue, and people whose absence had survived every change of authority.

The woman who had asked about her brother came after midday. The pharmacist recognized a folded copy of the letter in her hand. The new office might be able to ask different questions. It would still have to begin with what was known, and it would have to tell her when there was no answer.

The baker arrived carrying a repaired chair. He said he wanted his stool back now that the room could afford to be properly furnished. The nurse laughed, and then stopped because she had not expected to hear herself do it.

Outside, somebody was taking the old notices down. The porter asked them to leave the opening hours. The paper was worn and the ink had faded, but there had been mornings when keeping it on the door was the only promise the people inside could still make.
""",'has_global_flag = ac_camp_liberation_success has_country_flag = ac_camp_scene_1_delivered',glyph='medical')

scene(60,'OSE','The receiving island',"""
The duty officer found a training schedule beneath the new defense instructions. Someone had crossed out the afternoon exercise without removing the names of the crews assigned to it. For a moment the two documents seemed to describe the same day in different countries.

Wardog's place in the crisis would be shaped by decisions made around it. The completed island preparations meant warning coverage, a receiving plan and aircraft that were actually deployed. They did not make the young crews familiar with everything they were about to encounter. The mechanics had begun asking which faults could wait, and the controller had begun answering questions with shorter sentences.

A pilot came through the door looking for the person who could confirm a call. He had written the words down immediately after landing. The officer read them, asked who else had heard them, and put the paper with the originals. Nobody suggested that the pilot should improve his recollection to match the account already being circulated.

Outside, a vehicle stopped beside the fuel point. The driver had brought an item the receiving staff had requested before the situation changed. They unloaded it anyway. The island would still need ordinary supplies after the first emergency had acquired a name.

The training schedule remained beneath the orders when the officer left. He had intended to take it down. Then he thought of the people coming on duty who might need to remember that the afternoon had once contained something as modest as an exercise that could be postponed.
""",'has_global_flag = ac_camp_island_success',glyph='runway')

scene(61,'YUK','The distance below the surface',"""
The receiving officer read the operational return in a compartment too narrow for the second chair. The man delivering it remained standing, one shoulder turned toward the door so that someone carrying equipment could still pass.

The maritime denial preparations had been completed. That was the sentence intended for the command account. The attached pages contained the part that interested the officer: communications limits, withdrawal instructions, and the assumptions made by people who would not be present when a crew discovered that an assumption had failed.

He asked which instructions had been confirmed independently. The answer referred him to a message whose author was several offices away. He requested the original and watched the messenger decide whether that was an accusation.

There were many ways to be brave in a vessel that disappeared beneath the sea. Asking for an inconvenient confirmation was less visible than the others and could leave a longer mark on a career. The officer signed the completed work, retained its limitations, and refused to turn the difference between a credible plan and an inevitable success into a question of national loyalty.

Above them, the war would be described through large distances and dramatic names. Here the distance between the last clear instruction and the next uncertain reply could be measured by the length of a compartment.

The messenger took the signed copy away. The receiving officer kept the original on the desk until the end of the watch, where anyone asking for an assurance would have to see the conditions attached to it.
""",'has_global_flag = ac_camp_denial_success',glyph='submarine')

scene(62,'OSE','The copy without the correction',"""
The investigator placed the two messages side by side and asked the clerk which one had arrived first. The clerk answered immediately, then looked at the door as though the question had acquired a meaning only after he spoke.

The discrepancy was small. One receiving time, one alteration to a distribution list. Neither page contained enough information to support the conclusions that people would eventually want from it. Together they were sufficient to show that the current account could not be accepted without further work.

Protecting the evidence had cost capacity that might have been used for a simpler military task. It had also committed the command to protecting the people who could explain the difference between the papers. A successful investigation could not be measured entirely by the number of documents collected if its witnesses learned that answering a question made them easier to find.

The clerk asked whether he should prepare another corrected copy. The investigator said no. For the first time in the conversation, the answer sounded easy.

Across the room, a telephone rang until someone in another office picked it up. The investigator imagined how many conversations could fit between the request for an order and the order reaching the aircraft expected to obey it. A network did not need to control every voice. It needed a few voices to be treated as beyond question.

He wrote the clerk's receiving account on a separate sheet and explained where it would be kept. The explanation took longer than the discovery. It was the part on which the next witness would depend.
""",'has_global_flag = ac_camp_evidence_success',[dict(label='Protect the witness and circulate the contradiction.',effect='set_global_flag = ac_camp_evidence_public'),dict(label='Keep the inquiry inside a protected channel.',effect='set_global_flag = ac_camp_evidence_protected')],'document')

scene(63,'OSE','A crew held in reserve',"""
The rescue controller had been asked to justify the reserve twice. On the second occasion he put the original allocation beside the proposed alternative and invited the staff officer to sign the change. The officer said that was not necessary. The controller waited until he left before putting the papers away.

The crew had brought Chopper back. In the first confused receiving account, his name had appeared without an explanation of what it had taken to keep a recovery possible. The controller added the reserve allocation to the file. Keeping that possibility available had meant holding people and equipment out of other useful work. It had required somebody to make the argument before anyone could know which name would depend on it.

The crew chief checked the receiving instructions again. A rescue plan was not a promise that every aircraft could be reached. Weather, damage, distance and the person at the controls would still matter. The new route recorded what had actually been prepared and the alternate outcome it made possible; it did not rewrite the earlier allocation after the need became obvious.

Outside the office, a pilot was making an ordinary complaint about a piece of equipment. The controller listened to the familiar rhythm of the argument and felt suddenly unwilling to interrupt it.

He added the reserve crew's names to the duty return. If their work became necessary, the eventual report would probably compress the preparations into a phrase about a timely response. He wanted a record that the response had required time, people, and a refusal to spend them somewhere else.
""",'has_global_flag = ac_camp_chopper_preserved',glyph='medical')

scene(64,'OSE','The chair nobody moved',"""
The crew room did not look altered enough. A cup remained where it had been put down. Someone had left a note about the next duty change, and the person reading it folded the paper instead of correcting the name.

Chopper had not returned from the ceremony attack. The controller had asked for the receiving account to be checked, then asked again because he could not make himself accept that a second answer would resemble the first. Somewhere on another desk was the earlier allocation request. Nobody in the room needed it brought out to explain what the chair meant.

The squadron's next briefing arrived before anyone had decided what to do with the chair. That was the detail the duty officer would remember: the ordinary schedule continuing to supply its own answers. There were aircraft to inspect, people to receive, and questions that could be postponed only by giving them to somebody else.

A mechanic came in to ask for a signature. He stopped when he saw the room and began to leave. One of the pilots took the paper from him. Neither man looked at the place where the cup stood.

The loss would remain in the later story. It would change who spoke, what a promise of protection sounded like, and how easily another request for trust could be made. It would not appear as a penalty that disappeared with a timed national spirit.

The duty officer left the chair where it was for the rest of the evening. Moving it could wait until someone had a reason better than making the room look prepared for the next briefing.
""",'has_global_flag = ac_camp_chopper_lost',glyph='document')

scene(65,'YUK','The pencil at the receiving table',"""
The orderly had borrowed the pencil from a child. He had intended to return it when the receiving table obtained its own supplies, but the child kept moving through the queue with her family, and every time he looked up she was a little farther away.

The homeland evacuation effort was complete in the operational return. The people receiving its consequences were still arriving. Some had papers from an office that no longer answered its telephone. Others had a remembered address and the names of relatives who might have left before them. The officer at the table had learned to write Unknown without making it look like a refusal to ask.

Across the road, a military vehicle was being redirected toward the actual invasion front. Its passengers watched the queue while their driver argued about priorities. The receiving officer could see both needs and could satisfy neither by pretending the other was less real.

He entered the completed work alongside its limits. There was no new territorial possession to announce. There was a receiving line that had been funded, staffed and kept operating while the front made the next day's journey uncertain.

Near evening, the child returned for the pencil. The orderly apologized and offered to find another. She told him to keep it until the queue was finished.

He looked down the road. Then he wrote her family's receiving number on the back of their paper, carefully enough that they would be able to read it after the fold had worn thin.
""",'has_global_flag = ac_camp_evacuation_success',glyph='refugees')

scene(66,'OSE','An authenticated voice',"""
The operator asked the question twice because an ordinary mistake would have been easier to understand than a convincing answer. The reply matched the verification record. He looked at the officer beside him and repeated the result without adding anything that might become a rumor on its way across the room.

The rescue had succeeded. Harling's voice supplied an authority the compromised channels had tried to remove, but it did not make every listener ready to trust the next message. The operator kept the verification record beside the original, aware that relief could make people careless with the very procedures that had allowed them to recognize the voice in the first place.

The investigators began checking the circulation before the celebratory calls had ended. A government could be present and still have its instructions diverted. A squadron could be vindicated and still have lost people who would not hear the vindication.

Someone asked whether the crew should be brought into the room. The receiving officer said they should first be allowed to finish the work that had brought them back. The answer disappointed a man who had already begun arranging the photograph.

Outside, the military schedule continued to run. Across the ocean, another government had its own missing authority and its own reasons to doubt that Osea could offer a reliable agreement. Restoring one voice opened the possibility of an answer. It did not supply the answer itself.

The operator made a copy of the verified transmission and marked the original. This time, if somebody changed the distribution, there would be a record from which the change could be read.
""",'has_global_flag = ac_camp_harling_success',glyph='communications')

scene(67,'YUK','The right to answer',"""
The first question put to the returning authority was about a broadcast. It should have been about his condition, and the man asking knew that before the words were out. There were units waiting for an order they could recognize and people already deciding what would count as proof that it had come freely.

The operation had restored Nikanor's political voice to the crisis. It had not made the military's earlier decisions cease to exist. The receiving staff began assembling an account of who had been instructed to do what, and which instructions had passed through offices that now preferred not to answer the telephone.

An officer asked whether Osea would reciprocate. Nobody in the room was entitled to say that it would. The reply would have to come from a present authority on the other side, through a channel whose record could be kept by both governments.

The rescued man listened while the conditions were explained. The people doing the explaining had spent much of the war discovering how a condition could be removed from a message without altering its appearance. This time they wanted the limits spoken clearly enough to survive the room.

Outside, the queue at the receiving table had grown again. News of a possible end could make ordinary waiting harder, because it asked people to imagine the journey home before anyone had opened the road.

The officer preparing the next transmission left space for an actual answer. He would enter refusal, acceptance or silence when one of them occurred. A political restoration deserved at least that much honesty from the paperwork following it.
""",'has_global_flag = ac_camp_nikanor_success',glyph='assembly')

scene(68,'OSE','The platform they had shared',"""
An older technical folder was brought into the operations room to answer a current targeting question. Its cover belonged to the period when the Arkbird had represented cooperation over the debris left above the world. The officer opening it paused at the names on the distribution list.

The platform had been committed to the war and then taken into hands its original partners could no longer trust. The interception crews had been asked to stop it. Now the technical staff were working through the return, checking what had been destroyed and which facilities below would still expect instructions from a machine that could no longer send them.

The technical officer explained the limits of the current information. Someone asked whether the old cooperative record helped. It did, in ways that made several people around the table uncomfortable. Knowledge once shared to keep a platform working could also help determine how to stop it.

No one tried to solve that discomfort with a speech. The crew needed a briefing it could use. The investigators needed to preserve the trail of control. The government would eventually need to explain how an object that had carried one kind of promise had acquired another kind of mission.

After the report, the older folder remained on the table beside the current return. A clerk asked where it should be filed. The technical officer told him to keep the two together. The transition between them was part of the result, and would be the easiest part for a later summary to lose.
""",'has_global_flag = ac_camp_arkbird_success',glyph='air')

scene(69,'OSE','The last plot on the screen',"""
The room had exhausted the ordinary ways of asking for an update. Nobody wanted to interrupt the operators, and everybody wanted to know whether the last report meant what they hoped it meant. The officer standing behind the plotting desk folded his arms, unfolded them, and finally put his hands on the back of an empty chair.

The verified return supplied the answer they had been waiting for. The last plot on the screen no longer represented a threat descending toward a city. Beyond the operations room, people who had no reason to know the crews' names would continue with an evening that those crews had just helped preserve.

The controller passed the verified return to the receiving officer. Around them, the command began to separate the questions that had depended on the interception from those that had merely been waiting behind it. There were forces still fighting. There was another government whose acceptance could not be entered on the strength of an allied success. There were investigators whose evidence had become more vulnerable precisely because so many people wanted the crisis to be over.

A technician sat down abruptly, as if remembering that the chair had been there all along. Someone brought water. The ordinary gesture spread through the room with an efficiency no instruction had managed during the previous hour.

The officer began preparing the reciprocal terms. He left the field marked Accepted empty. The next stage required two governments to agree on what they could enforce, and the operators had just supplied a powerful reason to stop pretending that an intended outcome was the same thing as a completed one.
""",'has_global_flag = ac_camp_solg_success',glyph='satellite')

scene(80,'BLK','The country inside the file',"""
The archivist had arranged the settlement records by the authority that issued them. The visiting officer asked whether they could be rearranged by consequence. The archivist considered that for a moment and said it would require a much larger table.

Belka's earlier war had left a particular country behind. The records retained its actual victory, narrow peace or defeat rather than replacing it with the outcome most convenient to the current argument. The veterans waiting outside the room did not all remember the same event as the beginning of what had followed.

The officer opened a folder of receiving orders. A signature appeared several times in offices that no longer existed under the same names. He asked who had inherited the responsibilities. The archivist answered with a list of institutions, then with a list of people, and finally with the admission that the two lists no longer matched.

There were patriotic explanations for the gaps. There were also men whose influence depended on keeping the explanations simple. A government could listen to its veterans without granting every person speaking for them a right to conduct policy in secret.

The officer requested another copy of the distribution trail. Outside, someone laughed at a remark he could not hear. For an instant the sound made the waiting room seem ordinary.

The archivist brought the larger table before he brought the next folder. He had decided to take the question about consequences seriously, even though it would make the work less convenient for everyone who had expected to finish before lunch.
""",'has_global_flag = ac_camp_archive_success',glyph='document')

scene(81,'BLK','The witness who needed a door',"""
The witness asked which entrance he should use when he returned. The investigator had expected a question about protection, and realized that this was one. A man could be promised confidentiality in a room whose only door opened onto an office where everybody knew his face.

The independent investigation had completed its work. Its evidence could corroborate the foreign inquiry without requiring either foreign government to accept an unsupported assurance from Belka. That was why the witness mattered, and why keeping him alive and reachable had become a political choice rather than an administrative detail.

He had brought a procurement copy with the receiving marks still visible. No single line explained the network. The connections between the marks did. An order had been made convenient, an objection had been redirected, and a request had acquired the appearance of authority as it moved.

The investigator explained where the testimony would go and what could not be promised. The witness listened more closely to the limits than to the assurances. He had spent years hearing assurances offered by people who retained the power to forget them.

Beyond the window, staff were leaving for the evening. The ordinary traffic of the institution made the investigation's protection look fragile. It depended on people continuing to make inconvenient decisions after the immediate argument had ended.

The investigator walked the witness to a different entrance. Tomorrow there would still be questions about the file. At least one answer could be supplied before the man had to ask again.
""",'has_global_flag = ac_camp_witness_success',glyph='scales')

scene(82,'BLK','A channel without a letterhead',"""
The first useful reply arrived without a formal heading. The handler read it twice, then checked the receiving details against the promise that had been made in another office. The words were ordinary enough to file under routine correspondence. Their usefulness lay in who had agreed to make them arrive.

Building the channel had spent real capacity. People had been taken from visible work to maintain relationships that would not appear in the official account. The government supporting that effort could still describe itself as separate from the network, but the distinction was becoming a decision it had to enforce rather than a fact it could assume.

The handler's contact asked what would be expected next. He answered only the portion needed for the current arrangement. Compartmentalization protected the plan from discovery and protected its participants from having to judge the whole of it. Neither protection would survive an honest reconstruction of the correspondence.

On the desk beside the message was a veterans' invitation. The handler had not decided whether to attend. At such gatherings, the language of a country's grievance could make a private ambition sound like a duty shared by everyone in the room.

He put the invitation in a drawer and prepared the next request. The receiving office would see a limited administrative favor. Somewhere else, a clerk might keep the original, a witness might remember the change, or an investigator might ask why the same hand kept appearing between unrelated orders.

The channel was working. That was also the moment it began leaving something worth finding.
""",'has_global_flag = ac_camp_network_success',glyph='communications')

scene(83,'BLK','The access they called control',"""
The confirmation reached the room after several minutes in which nobody admitted to waiting. The handler checked the authentication and placed it where the others could see. Their strategic access operation had succeeded before the investigations could prevent it.

The network had obtained a foothold in the command channel it sought. It had done so by spending resources, preserving contacts and accepting an exposure trail that could still bring consequences after the immediate operation. The handler had made a habit of calling each request a limited favor. With the confirmation in front of him, the combined weight of those favors was becoming difficult to describe in the same reassuring language.

One of the men described the result as control. The technician corrected him with visible reluctance. Access depended on an operator continuing to obey, on a system continuing to respond, and on other people not changing the conditions while the room congratulated itself. The handler let the correction stand. It made the success less satisfying and the next decision more accurate.

Across the ocean, the war had not acquired a new belligerent merely because this room had found a way to influence it. Belka's population had not voted for the operation, and the country's conventional forces had not received an order to enter the conflict.

The handler began the continuation account. It would record what the network actually preserved through the final crisis. A moment of access was not yet an ending. The people in the room would have to live through the difference, along with people who had never been told that the choice existed.
""",'has_global_flag = ac_camp_seizure_success',glyph='satellite')

scene(70,'OSE','The names left off the return',"""
The receiving officer had been instructed to expect a movement that would not appear in the ordinary duty return. He read the instruction, checked its authority, and asked for an original copy to be retained somewhere outside the office that had sent it.

Wardog's departure had succeeded. The people protecting it had broken with a compromised command in ways that could not be repaired by a later explanation of good intentions. A clerk could be accused of losing a document. A controller could be accused of recognizing the wrong voice. The flight crews would bear accusations large enough to make those smaller choices easy to forget.

In the receiving room, someone asked which names should be entered. The officer answered that the private list should be complete. An absent name could become an excuse to deny that a person had ever needed protection.

The aircraft brought work with them. A mechanic wanted a current fault return. Another asked where the next service instruction would come from. The officer had spent the day preparing for a political crisis and found himself relieved by questions that admitted the possibility of an ordinary answer.

He sent the crews toward the people who could help them and remained beside the private receiving list. Outside, a pilot looked back at the closed door, then followed the others.

The departure had made a future possible without making it safe. Somewhere in the old command structure, the public return would be written. Here, at least, the people who had arrived would not have to rely on that return to prove that they existed.
""",'has_global_flag = ac_camp_breakout_success',glyph='air')

scene(71,'YUK','The person beyond the uniform',"""
The liaison did not begin by asking the officer to trust him. That was the first useful thing about the conversation. He gave a receiving detail that could be checked independently and waited while the operator did so.

Bartlett's place in the history of the war had made the idea of an independent contact easier for some men to imagine and harder for others to accept. The current receiving staff could not borrow his reputation as a substitute for doing their own work. They needed to know who had supplied a message and what could presently be confirmed about it.

The officer put the reply beside the original. There were people imprisoned behind the authority claimed by the current orders, and the recovery effort would fail if everyone asking a question was required to approach the office that had arranged the silence.

Across the table, the liaison described the limits of the contact. The operator wrote them down without improving them. A protected uncertainty could still be useful. An invented certainty would become another weapon in the hands of whoever learned to circulate it first.

When the conversation ended, neither man offered a declaration about the character of the other's nation. They had agreed to preserve one channel and to verify the next message before making another promise.

The officer watched the liaison leave. The uniform had been the easiest thing about him to recognize, and the least sufficient explanation for why the conversation had been possible.
""",'has_global_flag = ac_camp_liaison_success',glyph='communications')

scene(72,'OSE','A name received in confidence',"""
The new designation appeared on a small sheet passed between people who had learned to read an order's distribution before its content. Razgriz. The receiving officer said it once to make certain that he would not stumble over it when someone was listening for a different name.

The president had returned through a successful rescue, and the squadron's work now had an authority beyond the command that had tried to dispose of it. That changed the explanation the government could offer. It did not erase the memory of having been abandoned by people whose instructions had once sounded equally certain.

The mechanic at the end of the room asked which maintenance return should be used. The officer gave him the current reference and asked that the earlier copies be preserved. A new name could protect an operation. It could also become a convenient place to lose the obligations that had arrived under the old one.

Outside, the crew preparing the next flight was discussing a fault that had followed an aircraft across the change in designation. The argument sounded familiar enough to let the receiving officer breathe more easily. Some responsibilities remained legible when the politics around them had become difficult to recognize.

He placed the small sheet in the current file. The next orders would require courage, but they would also require the much quieter work of keeping an accurate record of what had been promised to the people expected to carry them out.
""",'has_global_flag = ac_camp_harling_success '+done('OSE',66),glyph='air')

scene(73,'OSE','The two receiving frequencies',"""
The operators had prepared for confusion. They had not prepared for how moving it would be to hear two services confirm the same instruction without making a threat out of the difference in their accents.

The mixed-force preparations were complete. The current Yuktobanian command had supplied a receiving arrangement, and the Osean staff had circulated it with the limits intact. The joint effort could therefore use an agreement that existed beyond the hope expressed in a broadcast.

A naval officer asked for confirmation of a unit's position. On the other frequency, his counterpart asked the same question in a different order. The operator waited until both answers could be repeated without contradiction. Men who had spent the war learning to identify a voice as hostile were being asked to recognize it as responsible for an adjoining piece of work.

The Kestrel's place in the original war record would be remembered alongside the political appeals and the final effort against the network. The current operators had a less complete view. They knew the instructions in front of them, the people who had answered, and the next report they needed to receive before another group of crews could proceed.

Near the end of the watch, someone brought food into the room. The operator covering the second frequency shook his head, listened, and reached for his pencil. The other man left the plate beside him rather than taking it away.

For the moment, both channels were working. Keeping them that way had become a shared responsibility.
""",'has_global_flag = ac_camp_joint_fleet_success',glyph='carrier')

scene(90,'OSE','The person who could answer back',"""
The receiving list had acquired a column for changed duties. The clerk stopped at Chopper's name long enough for the man behind him to ask whether there was a problem. There was not. That was the point, although the clerk could not explain why the ordinary answer had caught in his throat.

The war had ended, and Chopper was alive to disagree with the arrangements being made around him. The rescue had become a sentence in the final account. Its longer version remained in the recovery file, beside the reserve allocation and the names of the people who had been kept available before anyone knew they would be needed.

An officer asked whether the squadron could receive visitors. The answer depended on several people whose work the visitors would not notice unless it was absent: the receiving clerk, the mechanic, the person arranging a quiet place for a crew that had spent too much time being watched.

Outside, a conversation became laughter and then an argument about something small. The officer looked toward the door. He had become accustomed to interpreting every sudden sound as information about the military situation. Learning to hear an ordinary dispute would take longer than the announcement of peace.

The clerk finished the new duty entry. Keeping a person alive had not preserved the life he would have led before the war. It had preserved his part in deciding what would happen next. That was a less complete victory than the paperwork suggested and a much larger one than an empty space on the old list could have supplied.
""",'has_global_flag = ac_cpw_ended has_global_flag = ac_camp_chopper_preserved',glyph='peace')

scene(91,'OSE','The line in the final account',"""
The final account requested a summary of losses. The duty officer had prepared one and then found that he could not send it without another page. He placed Chopper's name at the top of that page and waited while the rest of the room completed its own work.

There were people who wanted the end of the war to make every earlier argument unnecessary. The officer understood the wish. He had also seen how easily an unfinished account could become an excuse to require the same thing from another crew under a different name.

The recovery preparations that might have changed the outcome had not been completed in time. That fact belonged in the review without becoming a claim that the people left behind had known precisely what the missing preparations would cost. Responsibility was difficult enough without adding an invented certainty to it.

A mechanic came to ask where the squadron's old records should be sent. The officer gave him the receiving address and asked that the personal notes remain with them. The mechanic nodded as though he had been waiting for somebody to say that part aloud.

The chair in the crew room had been moved by then. Nobody could remember which day it happened. Its absence no longer interrupted the room in the same way, but it still changed who spoke and what could be answered with an easy promise.

The officer attached the second page to the summary. Peace had made it possible to finish the account. It had not made the account smaller.
""",'has_global_flag = ac_cpw_ended has_global_flag = ac_camp_chopper_lost',glyph='document')

scene(92,'BLK','The favors that became a record',"""
The handler's final account used the word preserved. He had considered several alternatives and rejected them because each implied a completeness the surviving network could not honestly claim.

Its strategic access had survived the decisive investigations. The foreign governments had not obtained the corroboration that could have stopped it, and the domestic witnesses had not supplied an independent trail in time. This alternate outcome left the network with leverage and with people who now expected that leverage to be used on their behalf.

An official asked whether the result could be described publicly. The handler looked at the list of contacts. Most of them had been promised that their individual contribution was a limited favor. A public account of the combined work would reveal how many separate assurances had depended on nobody seeing the whole.

The official accepted the refusal and asked a smaller question. Which office should receive the next request? The handler gave him a name. He would have to remember that he had given it, because successful secrecy created obligations more efficiently than it created trust.

Outside, the country continued its ordinary business under a government that had not declared a new conventional war. The people going home had no reason to distinguish the public authority above the office door from the private authority that had been exercised inside it.

The handler sealed the account. The network had reached an ending it could exploit. It had not reached an ending that would release it from the people whose lives it had used to get there.
""",'has_global_flag = ac_camp_network_success_ending',glyph='security')

scene(93,'BLK','The originals kept elsewhere',"""
The witness asked whether the case was finished. The investigator said that the immediate operation had ended and watched him discover how much distance could remain between those two answers.

The network had not preserved the strategic success its supporters sought. In some offices the investigation had stopped a plan before it could acquire the appearance of authority. In others it had assembled enough of the surviving record for a private assurance to become a question somebody was required to answer in public.

There would be arguments about which description was correct. The investigator had already received several suggestions that a complete national reconciliation would be easier if certain details remained sealed. He asked whether the people proposing the seal intended to protect witnesses or to protect the institutions that had made the witnesses necessary.

The originals were being kept elsewhere. That arrangement had seemed inconvenient at the beginning of the inquiry and had become one of the reasons the inquiry survived. A second receiving point could not guarantee honesty. It could make dishonesty require another person to agree.

Outside the building, staff were returning the borrowed rooms to their ordinary purposes. The witness stood beside the door until the investigator asked whether he needed help with the journey home.

He said that he did. It was a smaller request than the one with which the case had begun, and the investigator was relieved to discover that he could give it a concrete answer without promising anything on behalf of the whole country.
""",'has_global_flag = ac_camp_network_exposed_ending',glyph='scales')

scene(33,'FCU','The callsign on the board',"""
The controller wrote Mobius 1 on the board in the same chalk he used for every other callsign. By the end of the watch, several people had found reasons to look at that part of the board again.

The completed Comona work gave the command a result it could use. Around it, reports had begun acquiring a different kind of significance. An enemy transmission repeated by an operator. An allied crew asking whether the same pilot would be present tomorrow. A maintenance officer discovering that a routine request drew attention from desks which usually wanted only the total number of serviceable aircraft.

The controller disliked the attention when it made people careless with their questions. A reputation could steady a crew. It could also encourage a commander to make a request whose weakness was concealed by the name expected to carry it.

He asked for the next assignment to be checked against the receiving plan. Nobody objected, although one officer looked disappointed by how ordinary the answer remained. There were fuel requirements, an inspection interval, and other pilots who would have to complete adjoining work for the operation to succeed.

Outside, the shift changed. The incoming operator read the board and paused at the same name. The controller handed over the actual tasking sheet before the pause could become an explanation.

The war was acquiring a legend. The next flight would still need a runway, a working aircraft and someone prepared to give an honest answer when it called home.
""",'has_global_flag = ac_camp_comona_success',glyph='air')

scene(34,'FCU','The last flight in the file',"""
The operations clerk had kept the earlier engineering folder because nobody had given him a receiving address for it. Now the final assault return lay beside it, making the delay look like foresight.

Megalith had been stopped. The completed operation had given the command an answer to a threat that had survived the military surrender. Mobius 1's callsign appeared among the records being assembled for the final account, beside the names of crews and staff whose work had been less easy to turn into a symbol.

The clerk began with the people who still required a receiving instruction. Some were going back to units that had changed their designation. Some would wait for a movement that the demobilization plan had not yet supplied. The announcement of victory had made all of them more impatient with a delay they would have accepted the day before.

A liaison from San Salvacion came to collect a copy of the settlement papers. He recognized the clinic reference in another folder and asked whether it could go with him. The clerk checked the distribution, made a receipt and watched the file acquire a journey beyond the operations room.

There were completed tasks on the desk and unfinished lives behind almost every one of them. Keeping the distinction clear had become the command's next obligation.

Before leaving, the clerk cleaned the old board. He copied the surviving receiving requirements onto a fresh sheet first. The callsigns could come down when the watch ended. The responsibilities attached to them would need somewhere else to go.
""",'has_global_flag = ac_camp_megalith_success',glyph='peace')

scene(94,'ERU','The last place on the board',"""
The receiving clerk could find no withdrawal order for the cadre. He checked the file again because the officer asking had phrased the request as though the order ought to be there.

The country had reached defeat without completing the arrangements that would have taken Yellow Thirteen away from the final defense. His loss would remain in this account. An authorization written after the settlement could help the people still waiting for transport, but it could not provide a route to someone whose last flight had already ended.

The maintenance chief came to collect the squadron's remaining papers. She placed the fault returns and the duty board copies in separate envelopes, then changed her mind and put them together. The people receiving the record would need to see what had been asked of the aircraft beside what had been asked of the crews.

Outside, a group of civilians was waiting to enter an office that had been military the previous week. The clerk could hear their questions through the open window. The building's responsibilities were changing more quickly than its staff could learn the new forms.

The officer thanked him for looking and remained beside the desk for another moment. There was nothing further the clerk could supply. He had become accustomed to recognizing that moment in a conversation and was still unsure how to end it kindly.

When the officer left, the clerk entered the receiving address on the combined envelope. The squadron's story would travel onward with its absences intact, among people who would have to decide what the country owed to the living.
""",'has_global_flag = ac_camp_yellow_thirteen_lost',glyph='document')

scene(74,'OSE','The joke before the engine',"""
Chopper was still talking when the mechanic pointed at the ladder. He put one hand against his chest with theatrical injury, as though a man being asked to climb into an aircraft had suffered an offense against the arts.

Grimm laughed before he looked toward Nagase. She was watching the crew at the receiving end of the runway, where a vehicle had stopped in a place it was not supposed to stop. The driver moved it after a signal. Nagase kept watching until the path was clear.

"You could try saying something reassuring," Chopper told her.

"The runway is clear."

That made Grimm laugh again. Chopper accepted the answer with a small bow that the flight suit made awkward. By then the mechanic had begun smiling too, although he tried to conceal it by checking something he had already checked.

Blaze was listening. The controller called for confirmation, and the small exchange dissolved into the ordinary sequence of people making certain that the next person had heard them. The island's preparations had given them somewhere to return. They still had to bring the aircraft back to it.

Nagase waited beside the ladder until the crew chief finished. Then she glanced across at the other aircraft. The joke had not made the task smaller. It had reminded the people preparing for it that the voices inside those machines belonged to people they knew.

When the first engine caught, Grimm looked toward the cleared runway once more. Chopper was finally quiet, busy with the work he had spent the previous minute pretending was an interruption.
""",'has_global_flag = ac_camp_island_success ac_cpw_war_running = yes date < 2010.11.29',glyph='air')

scene(75,'OSE','The sea on the windscreen',"""
Grimm kept describing the sea. Every time he reached the part he meant to explain, he began with a different detail, as though the right description of the water would make the rest of the sortie easier to understand.

Nagase let him finish. The first carrier-hunt operation had returned a usable result, and the debrief required a clear account of what the crews had actually observed. It did not require a younger pilot to discover the right words before somebody would give him a chair.

Chopper sat backward on one of the spare chairs and listened more closely than his posture suggested. When Grimm stopped, he asked a practical question about the receiving call. Grimm answered it at once. The relief on his face was so visible that Chopper looked away before offering the next question.

Outside, a mechanic was washing salt from a surface that had acquired a different significance to every person entering the hangar. To him it was a corrosion problem. To the crew it was something they had seen very close while trying to do their work above an ocean that offered no useful place to put a damaged aircraft.

The debriefing officer took the completed return and left the room. For a little while nobody followed him.

Grimm asked whether he had spoken too much. Chopper said that would be an impressive achievement in the present company. Nagase smiled and reached for the untouched water beside the younger pilot. He took it, and the conversation finally moved on to a part of the flight he could explain without beginning again at the sea.
""",'has_global_flag = ac_camp_submarine_success ac_cpw_war_running = yes date < 2010.11.29',glyph='submarine')

scene(76,'OSE','People who still knew the aircraft',"""
Pops listened to the mechanic's description of the fault before looking at the pilots. He had not come into the room to make a speech about trust. There was an aircraft to prepare, and the men and women standing around it needed a useful answer.

The escape had succeeded. The squadron had reached people willing to receive it, though the accusation following Wardog could travel along channels faster than any aircraft. Nagase had begun checking the receiving instructions for precisely that reason. Grimm remained beside the aircraft, looking relieved whenever somebody gave him a task whose meaning was clear.

The mechanic pointed out the part he could not release without another inspection. Pops nodded. Nothing in the day's politics had made an incomplete inspection more complete. The answer seemed to steady the room. For a few minutes, authority could be recognized in a person accepting responsibility for a specific piece of work.

Nagase came back with the verified contact. She waited until the mechanic had finished before speaking. Nobody asked her to make the confirmation sound more certain than it was.

Beyond the hangar, another crew was preparing to leave. The engines rose, settled and rose again. Grimm listened until the aircraft was clear of the receiving area, then turned back toward the people who still knew what needed doing beside him.

The squadron had lost the safety of an institution it could obey without question. It had not lost every person capable of giving an honest instruction. For the moment, that was enough to begin the next inspection.
""",'has_global_flag = ac_camp_breakout_success ac_cpw_war_running = yes check_variable = { num_deployed_planes > 0 } check_variable = { deployed_airforce_manpower_k > 0 }',glyph='tools')

scene(77,'FCU','The breath between calls',"""
The controller recognized the breathing before he recognized the silence. The channel was open, the aircraft was where the plot said it should be, and nobody was reporting a problem. That did not make the interval between calls feel empty.

Mobius 1 acknowledged the next instruction. The voice carried little of the mythology already beginning to gather around the callsign. There was a task ahead, a receiving plan behind, and other crews working in adjoining pieces of the same sky.

At the next desk, an operator was struggling to identify a report through interference. The controller waited for its position to be confirmed before changing the tasking. A confident guess would have been quicker. He had written enough corrections to know how long the consequences of a quick guess could last.

Outside the operations building, the wind was moving a loose sheet of metal against a wall. Someone went to secure it. The sudden absence of the tapping made the controller realize how much of the watch he had spent hearing it beneath the radio.

The reconnaissance commitment had been completed by aircraft actually put into the air. Its result would help the next operation, and the next crews would have to discover how much of it remained useful when the conditions changed.

He passed the verified report onward. Mobius 1 answered, and the interval opened again. The controller watched the plot and kept his next instruction ready, resisting the temptation to fill a difficult silence with words that the pilot did not need.
""",'has_global_flag = ac_camp_recon_success ac_ucw_war_running = yes check_variable = { num_deployed_planes > 0 } check_variable = { deployed_airforce_manpower_k > 0 }',glyph='communications')

scene(78,'ERU','A pilot at the kitchen table',"""
Yellow Four found Thirteen at a table too small for the meal and the district map laid beside it. He moved the map when she sat down. The gesture made room for her plate while leaving the edges of the marked roads visible beneath his elbow.

Outside, the garrison's newly authorized occupation arrangements were being explained to another patrol. The room was quiet enough to hear the repetition of an instruction through the open window. Four listened, then asked whether the patrol had understood it.

Thirteen said he hoped so.

"They will need more than that at the checkpoint."

He looked toward the window without answering immediately. The squadron's work offered a clearer measure of competence than the roads on the district map. A flight could return with a result. A written permission could arrive at a clinic and remain useless to the next man holding a rifle.

Four began eating. The ordinary task allowed them both to look away from a question neither could settle from the table. A vehicle stopped outside, and somebody called for the officer whose signature was on the passage order.

Thirteen moved the map again before standing. This time he put it completely off the table.

Four watched him go. There would be flying to do afterward, and a debrief at which someone would ask whether the squadron had fulfilled its orders. She wanted to believe that a good answer could still be recognized without first deciding which part of the day's orders the question was supposed to mean.
""",'has_global_flag = ac_camp_occupation_success ac_ucw_war_running = yes 310 = { is_controlled_by = ERU } NOT = { has_global_flag = ac_camp_yellow_four_settled }',glyph='security')

scene(79,'ERU','The engine nobody started',"""
Four stopped beside the aircraft and waited for the maintenance chief to look up. The chief kept working for several seconds because she wanted to finish the inspection entry before beginning the argument.

The reserve aircraft would remain in the hangar. The command had funded the maintenance period and accepted its immediate cost. The operations room had subsequently found several persuasive reasons why the original decision ought to be reconsidered.

"Are you going to tell me it is nearly ready?" Four asked.

"When it is ready."

The answer made Four smile despite herself. Beyond them, a crew was preparing another aircraft for work that would otherwise have been hers. She watched them longer than the chief expected.

There was no comfortable way to be the person whose safety required somebody else to undertake a useful task. There was also no honest way for a mechanic to repair that discomfort by releasing a machine she would not sign for. The chief explained what had been done and what still required inspection. Four asked questions that showed she had been listening through the argument.

When the pilot left, the chief went back to the same entry. The work had not advanced while they talked, but the conversation had made its purpose easier to recognize.

Outside, an engine started and settled into a familiar sound. The aircraft inside the hangar remained quiet. Keeping it that way had required an instruction, a paid period of work and two people willing to carry the awkwardness of a decision made before its consequences could be known.
""",'has_global_flag = ac_camp_maintenance_success ac_ucw_war_running = yes NOT = { has_global_flag = ac_camp_yellow_four_settled }',glyph='tools')

scene(95,'OSE','A complaint worth hearing',"""
Chopper objected to the receiving room's choice of chair before asking whether anybody had brought something to drink. The orderly was trying to decide which part of the complaint required a medical answer when Nagase came in and took the spare seat.

Grimm followed with the confidence of a man carrying out a task he had been able to understand completely. He put the drink where Chopper could reach it, then stood beside the door as though waiting for another instruction.

The rescue had brought their friend back. It had not supplied the old ease with which the squadron had once entered a room together. For a moment each of them seemed to be deciding which ordinary remark would survive the significance being placed on it.

Chopper made the decision for them by complaining about the chair again.

Nagase asked the orderly whether a different one could be found. The seriousness with which she asked finally made Chopper laugh. Grimm sat down. From the corridor came the sound of someone being redirected toward another receiving office, followed by a door closing too hard.

There would be later questions about duty, recovery and who had been held in reserve to make this return possible. They did not need to be answered during the first conversation in which the person at the center of them could interrupt.

The orderly went to look for another chair. When he returned, the three were discussing something he could not follow. He placed it beside them without asking for an explanation, pleased that the room had finally acquired a problem he could solve.
""",'has_global_flag = ac_camp_chopper_preserved ac_cpw_war_running = yes',glyph='medical')

scene(96,'YUK','The watch beneath the weather',"""
The weather reached the compartment as a change in the way loose objects behaved. A cup moved against the edge of its retaining bracket. Somewhere beyond the bulkhead, a man swore at a tool he had not secured properly.

The watch officer read the latest instruction beneath a light that made everyone look more tired than they admitted. The maritime denial effort was still active. The second carrier had neither been lost nor recalled, and the crew remained inside the interval between an order from above and whatever the next report from the sea would require.

One of the younger men asked how long they would remain on the current assignment. The officer gave him the confirmed part of the answer. He had learned to recognize the relief that followed a confident estimate and the resentment that followed its quiet replacement.

At the end of the compartment, a crewman was smoothing a letter against his knee. He had read it often enough that the folds were beginning to separate. When the officer looked over, the man put it away without being asked.

There were aircraft to maintain in a space that made every additional task involve someone else's passage through the same doorway. The watch continued through the weather, marked by ordinary requests and the small courtesies that kept them from becoming arguments.

The officer returned to the instruction. He would confirm what could be confirmed, preserve the limits, and give the next watch an account that would remain useful after the tone in which it was spoken had been forgotten.
""",'has_global_flag = ac_camp_denial_success ac_cpw_war_running = yes NOT = { has_global_flag = ac_camp_second_submarine_success } NOT = { has_global_flag = ac_camp_naval_reserve_success }',glyph='submarine')

PEACE="""The receiving operator kept the original beside the decoded text. There were officers in the room who would once have considered that an insult. Now none of them asked him to put it away.

The terms had arrived from an authenticated Osean authority after the political rescues and the completed final interception. They proposed a reciprocal cessation in the campaign's own war. The staff had checked the present participants. No wider conflict could be settled by treating this exchange as an instruction to governments that had never agreed to it.

Around the table, the practical questions began. Which orders would be sent first? Who would confirm receipt? What would happen if a unit received only half the exchange? The people asking had spent the war discovering how much damage could fit between an apparently clear instruction and the person expected to obey it.

The political representative waited until the military objections had been recorded. An agreement could survive an objection that had been heard. It was less likely to survive an assurance written over a question nobody had answered.

Outside, the duty staff were preparing another situation return. They would continue until they received a different order through a channel they could recognize. Hope had reached the building before authority to act on it, and the interval was becoming painful.

The offer remained open for a limited time. Acceptance would end the qualifying bilateral war only if its conditions still held when the answer was executed. Refusal would be recorded as refusal. Silence would remain silence. The operator placed a blank receiving sheet beside the original and waited for a decision that both countries would have to carry."""

PARTNER_SCENES={
    key:body for key,body in (
    ('rearbase',"The receiving request names a headquarters, but the island's return names beds, hangars and working shifts. North Point has prepared a bounded hosting commitment. Accepting it records present allied consent; it does not annex the island's responsibilities into a command chart. The receiving officer asks whether the local authority's limits have been circulated. The clerk says they are attached to the original. Nobody in the room has read the attachment yet. Before the answer goes back, somebody will have to decide whether an inconvenient condition is part of an offer or an obstacle to be removed from it."),
    ('transport',"The transport offer arrives with a limit written into its first paragraph. Amber has already paid for preparation. The coalition must now decide whether it can use the defined commitment on those terms. A staff officer suggests asking for a larger allocation. The liaison points out that such an answer would still be a refusal of the offer presently on the table. Behind the papers are drivers and depot staff whose time cannot be multiplied by improving the wording of a reply. Acceptance records a current receiving permission. It does not provide a second payment or a free stockpile."),
    ('dispersal',"The proposed receiving site is less convenient than the principal base. That is part of its purpose. Ugellas has prepared an additional commitment whose usefulness depends on the coalition respecting its capacity and conditions. The operations officer traces the movement on the map and asks whether the local service can remain open at night. The liaison turns to the attachment before answering. A permission to receive one defined commitment is not a promise to absorb every subsequent requirement. The reply will be returned to the government that actually paid for the preparations, where acceptance, refusal and silence remain different entries."),
    ('inspection',"The inspection docket contains a question directed at the command receiving it. A hurried acceptance could preserve the appearance of accountability while denying the inspectors the records they need. Delarus has funded a defined service and attached its requirements. The reviewing officer reads those before considering how the result might be described in the next coalition report. There is no reward merely for having been offered scrutiny. Accepting the commitment supplies present consent to the proposed work. Refusing it leaves the expense of preparation with the sender and the unresolved cases with the institutions responsible for answering them."),
    ('access',"The access offer has a mandate attached. Sapin's government has specified which work it can presently authorize and where its responsibility ends. The receiving officer has seen access described as a preliminary to larger commitments often enough to read the restriction first. A clear acceptance would give the prepared operation a usable receiving permission. It would not create a base, an alliance or a declaration of war. The liaison asks for the original mandate to remain with the reply so that the next office cannot mistake a limited consent for an invitation to send a different request under the same reference number."),
    ('sovereignty',"Ustio's offer is accompanied by a right to withdraw. The officer reviewing it pauses at that clause. To planners trying to produce a reliable return, a condition can look like a defect in the resource being offered. To the government making the commitment, it is the reason the offer can be made at all. Accepting it records permission for a defined, funded mission and preserves its stated limits. It does not create a guarantee or borrow an earlier settlement to justify a new obligation. The receiving clerk keeps space for an answer, including an answer the sender would rather not receive."),
    ('transit',"The transit papers describe an inspected movement. Gebet has completed preparations on its side and asks the receiving authority to confirm the arrangement. The council's transport officer wants the reference entered in the current schedule. The civil liaison asks whether the inspection requirements will travel with it. Neither question supplies the other's answer. Acceptance permits the bounded operation to complete while its present conditions hold. Refusal closes the offer without refunding work already performed. The road remains in the country whose staff must maintain it, regardless of how many arrows appear on the regional command's copy of the map."),
    ('contribution',"The contribution is deliberately smaller than the preliminary request. Recta's government has funded what it can offer while retaining its domestic recovery obligations. The council can accept the defined amount or refuse it; it cannot make the larger proposal real by thanking the sender enthusiastically. An official asks whether the restriction should be mentioned in the public account. The liaison says that mentioning a limit before it is reached is one of the few ways an agreement can remain useful afterward. The receiving permission records present consent and carries no automatic alliance, guarantee, resource transfer or repayment."),
)}

